FOUND IN A CURL THAT LOOKED FINE

  {'count': 11, 'facts_verified_at': '2026-09-05', 'updated_at': '2026-09-05'}

The count was right. Both dates were 2026-09-05, five days before twenty-six
New York markets, forty-four pages and a new corridor landed.

TWO DATES THAT MEAN DIFFERENT THINGS

`facts_verified_at` is the day a person last re-read the NAP, hours, founding
year and price bands on the live host. It is CORRECTLY 2026-09-05 and is not
touched here: nobody has re-read production since, and moving it to look fresh
is the exact dishonesty its own comment forbids. It is the field an agent reads
to decide whether the phone number can be trusted.

`updated_at` is the freshest thing in the registry — what a consumer keys a
cache on. It was computed from reviews, prices and evidence and NOT from
locations, so it did not move through the largest factual change this site has
had. Anything caching on it would still be serving the old territory, which on a
site whose entire recent work is territory is the most expensive possible place
for a stale signal.

PER-MARKET PROVENANCE

Every location also carried the coverage claim's single date. That was adequate
while coverage moved as one block and stopped being true the moment individual
markets were confirmed on their own dates. Buffalo's operational position was
confirmed on 2026-09-10 and the API said 2026-09-05 about it.

Each location now carries its own market's confirmation date:

  facts_verified_at 2026-09-05 | updated_at 2026-09-10
    buffalo    2026-09-10   published
    pittsford  2026-09-10   published
    oakville   2026-09-09   published
    toronto    2026-09-09   region

An agent deciding whether to trust a coverage statement reads exactly that
field. Telling it the answer was verified before the confirmation that created
it is worse than telling it nothing.

Four tests hold both dates apart: updated_at may never be older than the newest
market confirmation, facts_verified_at may never be dragged along with it, every
location reports its own market's date, and a market confirmed later reports a
later date than one confirmed earlier — which is the thing one shared date could
not say.

66 guards green, 336 tests, tsc at its 115-error baseline.

